  "slug": "combination-sum-iv",
  "title": "Combination Sum IV",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Knapsack",
  "patterns": [
    "knapsack",
    "unbounded knapsack",
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "despite the name, this is a DP problem, not a backtracking-and-collect-all-sequences problem - the answer only asks for a count",
    "define dp[t] as the number of ordered sequences summing to exactly t, with dp[0] = 1 (one way to make 0: the empty sequence)",
    "for a given t, the last number placed could be any num in nums, so dp[t] = sum over every num <= t of dp[t - num]",
    "since order matters here, the target must be the outer loop and nums the inner loop - the opposite nesting from Coin Change II, which is exactly what turns 'combinations' into 'permutations counted by DP'",
    "build dp from 0 up to target, trying every num at every t - dp[target] is the final answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using the Coin Change II loop order (coins outer, target inner), which counts unordered combinations instead of ordered sequences and undercounts badly",
    "trying to enumerate every sequence explicitly with backtracking, which is correct but far too slow for larger targets when only a count is needed",
    "forgetting dp[0] = 1, which silently zeroes out every dp[t] that depends on it"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of distinct positive integers, `nums`, and a `target`\nvalue. A number may be reused any number of times, and order matters, so\n(1, 2) and (2, 1) count as different sequences.\n\n**Return the number of ordered sequences of `nums` that sum to exactly `target`.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 200",
      "1 <= nums[i] <= 1000",
      "all values in nums are distinct",
      "1 <= target <= 1000"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a number in nums is larger than target (it can never be used)",
      "target cannot be reached by any sequence (return 0)",
      "a single-element nums list"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
